use web
use json
use core
use md
into panes.he
lang he

The module is namespace `panes`. It owns the tags 500 to 599, all i32 constants: `T_SPLIT` is 501 (click on the element "split-btn"), `T_STREAM` is 502 (chat streams), `T_MODELS` is 503 (the fetch of "/api/models"), `T_KEY` is 504 (keydown on a pane textarea), `T_INPUT` is 505 (input on a pane textarea), `T_DOWN` is 507 (mousedown on a divider), `T_MOVE` is 508 (mousemove on the document) and `T_UP` is 509 (mouseup on the document).

A pane has an id, an element handle and a handle of its messages element. The module keeps three parallel module lists in creation order: `pane_ids` of integers, `pane_els` of i32 handles and `pane_msgs` of i32 handles. `container` is a module i32 handle. A pane's chat session id lives in the attribute "data-session" of its element and is absent until the server sends one. Pane element handles and messages handles are never released, because running requests may still use them.

A chat request has its stream request id, the handle of its thinking div, the handle of its messages element and the handle of its pane element. The module keeps four parallel module lists for the requests that are running: `rq_ids` of integers, `rq_think`, `rq_msgs` and `rq_pane` of i32 handles. A request whose id is minus 1 has ended. The text a request has collected lives in attributes of its thinking div: "data-buf" holds the received event text not yet processed, "data-tools" holds the tool badges built so far, "data-final" holds the final message and "data-got" is "1" once a message event has arrived. `mq_ids` of integers and `mq_sels` of i32 handles are two parallel module lists for the running "/api/models" fetches: the fetch id and the select element handle waiting for it.

The module also keeps the state of a divider drag: `dragging` (bool, false), `drag_ready` (bool, false: the document listeners exist), `drag_div`, `drag_left` and `drag_right` (i32 handles), `drag_x`, `drag_lw` and `drag_rw` (real numbers: the mouse x, the left pane width and the right pane width at the start).

`pane_index(id)` returns the position of the first pane in `pane_ids` with that id, or minus 1.

`auto_resize(h)` sets the style "height" of element h to "auto" and then to the smaller of its "scrollHeight" number property (web `get_num`) and 150, written as a whole number followed by "px".

`scroll_down(h)` sets the number property "scrollTop" of element h to its "scrollHeight" number property.

`add_msg(msgs, kind, text)` creates a div with class "msg " followed by kind and the text content `text`, appends it to element msgs and releases its handle.

`pane_html(out, id)` appends the markup of a pane to `out`: the text `<div class="pane-header"><span>`, `core::t` "pane", a space, the id plus 1, the text `</span><select class="pane-model" data-change="panes.model"></select><button class="pane-close" data-act="panes.close" data-arg="`, the id, the text `" title="`, `core::t` "close_pane", the text `">✕</button></div><div class="chat-col" style="border:none"><div class="messages" id="pane-msgs-`, the id, the text `"><div class="welcome"><h2>`, `core::t` "welcome_title", the text `</h2><p>`, `core::t` "welcome_desc", the text `</p></div></div><div class="input-area"><div class="input-wrap"><textarea rows="1" placeholder="`, `core::t` "placeholder_chat", the text `"></textarea><button class="send-btn" data-act="panes.send" data-arg="`, the id, and the text `"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M2.01 21L23 12 2.01 3 2 10l15 2-15 2z"/></svg></button></div></div></div>`. The translated pieces are inserted as they are, not escaped.

`populate_models(id)` finds the pane with `pane_index`, stops when there is none, takes the first element matching ".pane-model" inside the pane element, stops when there is none, starts `web::fetch` of "/api/models" with tag `T_MODELS`, and pushes the fetch id and the select handle onto `mq_ids` and `mq_sels`.

`models_done()` is called when a "/api/models" fetch ends. It takes the fetched body with `fetch_take` of `ev_id()` and frees it with `fetch_free`. It finds the fetch id in `mq_ids`, stops when it is not there, takes the select handle and removes that entry from both lists by moving every later entry down one place and popping the last. When the HTTP status of the event (`ev_status()`) is not 0 and the body parses as JSON with a "models" member that is an array, it sets the inner HTML of the select to one option per array element, in order: the text `<option value="`, the element as text passed through `md::esc_html`, the text `">`, the same escaped text again and the text `</option>`, where an element that is not a string counts as empty text. In every other case it sets the inner HTML of the select to `<option>Error</option>`.

`create_pane()` stops with the toast of `core::t` "split_view" followed by ": max 3" when there are already 3 or more panes. Otherwise the new id is the current number of panes. It creates a div with class "pane-divider" and the attributes "data-left-pane" (the id minus 1 as text) and "data-right-pane" (the id as text), listens on it for "mousedown" with tag `T_DOWN` and the flag `web::PREVENT`, appends it to `container` and releases its handle. Then it creates a div with class "pane", the attribute "id" set to "pane-" followed by the id and the attribute "data-pane-id" set to the id, sets its inner HTML to the result of `pane_html`, and appends it to `container`. It listens on the first "textarea" inside the pane for "keydown" with tag `T_KEY` and for "input" with tag `T_INPUT`, both with no flags. It pushes the id, the pane handle and the handle of the first element matching ".messages" inside the pane onto the three pane lists, calls `populate_models` with the id, and shows the toast of `core::t` "split_view" followed by " → " and the id plus 1.

`close_pane(id)` does nothing when there is only one pane or when `pane_index` finds none. Otherwise it takes the pane's element and finds its position k among the children of `container`, walking them with `web::child` and comparing with `web::same`; a child that is not the pane element and has the class "pane-divider" is released right away. When k is above 0 and child k minus 1 has the class "pane-divider" it removes that child with `web::remove` and releases it. Otherwise, when child k plus 1 exists and has the class "pane-divider" it removes and releases that one. A child that is not a divider is not released. Then it removes the pane element with `web::remove`, removes the pane from the three pane lists by moving every later entry down one place and popping the last, and shows the toast `core::t` "close_pane".

`send_pane(id, ta)` sends the text of textarea ta to the pane with that id. It reads the "value" property of ta, trims it with `md::trim_js` and stops when nothing is left. It sets the "value" property of ta to empty and calls `auto_resize` on ta. It finds the pane with `pane_index` and stops when there is none. Its messages handle is the matching entry of `pane_msgs`. It removes the first element matching ".welcome" inside the messages element when there is one. It calls `add_msg` with kind "user" and the trimmed text, then `scroll_down`. It creates a div with class "msg assistant" whose inner HTML is the text `<div class="thinking"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>`, appends it to the messages element and calls `scroll_down`. It builds the JSON body text with the json module: an object with "message" (the trimmed text), "session_id" (the pane element's attribute "data-session" as a string, or null when the attribute is absent) and "working_dir" (the "value" property of the element "cfg-dir"), in that order. It starts the chat stream with `web::stream` using the url "/api/chat", method "POST", the headers text `{"Content-Type":"application/json"}`, that body and tag `T_STREAM`, and pushes the returned request id, the thinking div handle, the messages handle and the pane element handle onto the four request lists.

`finish_request(slot)` ends the request at position slot: it removes its thinking div with `web::remove`, releases that handle, and sets its id in `rq_ids` to minus 1. When every request id is then minus 1 it clears the four request lists.

`handle_sse(slot, kind, data)` handles one event of the request at position slot, where kind is the event name text and data its data text, and returns false when the request has ended and true otherwise. It parses data as JSON into a local `json::Doc` and returns true when it does not parse. For the kind "session" it sets the attribute "data-session" of the request's pane element to the "session_id" member when that is a string, and removes the attribute when it is not. For "tool_result" it takes the "tool" member as text (empty when it is not a string) and appends to the "data-tools" attribute of the thinking div the text `<span class="tool-badge `, "success" when the "status" member is the string "success" and otherwise "error", the text `">⚡ `, the tool name passed through `md::esc_html` and the text `</span>`; and when the tool name is exactly "write_file" or "edit_file" it posts the action "chat.parse_diff" with the whole data text as argument using `core::post`. For "message" it sets the "data-final" attribute to the "message" member when that is a string (empty text otherwise) and "data-got" to "1". For "error" it takes the "error" member as text when it is a non-empty string and the text "Unknown" otherwise, calls `finish_request` for the slot, calls `add_msg` on the request's messages element with kind "system" and the text `core::t` "error_prefix" followed by ": " and that text, and returns false. For "interrupted" it calls `finish_request`, then `add_msg` with kind "system" and the text `core::t` "generation_interrupted", and returns false. Any other kind returns true.

`on_chunk()` handles a stream chunk of tag `T_STREAM`. The chunk id is `ev_id()` and the request id is `ev_status()`. It takes the chunk bytes with `fetch_take` and frees them with `fetch_free`. It finds the request id in `rq_ids` and stops when it is not there. It reads the thinking div's "data-buf" attribute into a local buffer (empty when absent), appends the chunk bytes, and then repeatedly calls `core::sse_take` on that buffer. For each event it skips it when its data text is empty and otherwise calls `handle_sse` with the slot, the event name and the data text; when that returns false it stops without doing anything more. When all complete events are handled it writes the remaining buffer back to the "data-buf" attribute.

`on_end()` handles the end of a stream, tag `T_STREAM` with kind `web::EV_FETCH`. The request id is `ev_id()` and the HTTP status is `ev_status()`. It frees the fetched body with `fetch_take` and `fetch_free`. It finds the request id in `rq_ids` and stops when it is not there. When the status is 0 it calls `finish_request` and then `add_msg` on the request's messages element with kind "system" and the text `core::t` "connection_error". Otherwise it reads the thinking div's attributes "data-tools", "data-final" and "data-got" before calling `finish_request`, and when the tools text is not empty or "data-got" is present it creates a div with class "msg assistant" whose inner HTML is, when the tools text is not empty, the text `<div class="tool-group">`, the tools text and the text `</div>`, followed in every case by `md::render` of the final text; it appends that div to the messages element and releases its handle. Only in this case (the status is not 0) does it finish by calling `scroll_down` on the messages element; after a connection error it does not scroll.

`drag_start()` handles a mousedown on a divider, which is `web::ev_target()`. It adds the class "dragging" to it. It reads its attributes "data-left-pane" and "data-right-pane" as integers, finds the left and right panes with `pane_index`, and when either is missing or the attribute is not a number it releases the divider handle and stops. Otherwise it sets `drag_div` to the divider, `drag_left` and `drag_right` to the two pane elements, `drag_x` to `web::ev_x()`, and `drag_lw` and `drag_rw` to the widths of the two pane elements from `web::rect`, and sets `dragging` to true. The first time only (`drag_ready` false) it listens on `web::DOCUMENT` for "mousemove" with tag `T_MOVE` and for "mouseup" with tag `T_UP`, no flags, and sets `drag_ready` to true.

`drag_move()` does nothing unless `dragging`. The distance is `web::ev_x()` minus `drag_x`. The new left width is the larger of 280 and `drag_lw` plus the distance, and the new right width is the larger of 280 and `drag_rw` minus the distance. For each pane element it sets the style "flex" to "none" and the style "width" to the new width as a number followed by "px".

`drag_end()` does nothing unless `dragging`. It removes the class "dragging" from `drag_div`, releases that handle and sets `dragging` to false.

`key_down()` handles a keydown on a pane textarea, which is `web::ev_target()`. When the key (`web::ev_key`) is exactly "Enter" and the Shift modifier of `web::ev_mods()` is not set, it calls `web::ev_prevent`, takes the "data-pane-id" attribute, read as an integer, of the closest ancestor of the textarea with the class "pane" (use `web::closest`), and calls `send_pane` with that id and the textarea.

`init()` sets `container` to the element "pane-container". It pushes the first pane onto the three pane lists: id 0, the element "pane-0" and the handle of the first element matching ".messages" inside it. It listens for "click" with no flags on the element "split-btn" with tag `T_SPLIT`.

`event(tag, kind)` handles the module's tags. `T_SPLIT` calls `create_pane`. `T_STREAM` calls `on_chunk` when the kind is `web::EV_CHUNK` and `on_end` when it is `web::EV_FETCH`. `T_MODELS` calls `models_done` when the kind is `web::EV_FETCH`. `T_KEY` calls `key_down`. `T_INPUT` calls `auto_resize` on `web::ev_target()`. `T_DOWN` calls `drag_start`, `T_MOVE` calls `drag_move` and `T_UP` calls `drag_end`.

`act(verb, arg)` handles the queued actions of this module. "create" calls `create_pane`. "close" calls `close_pane` with the argument read as an integer. "send" reads the argument as an integer and calls `send_pane` with it and the first "textarea" inside the parent of `core::act_el`. "model" does nothing. Any other verb does nothing.
